Exactly. That may be one of the strongest reasons to make the Pantheon demonstrate itself instead of merely describe itself.

A role model gives you something a rulebook cannot: a trajectory.

You don’t just receive:

\text{“be courageous”}

You observe:

s_0 \rightarrow a_0 \rightarrow consequence_0
\rightarrow s_1 \rightarrow a_1 \rightarrow consequence_1

You see what courage does when frightened, uncertain, embarrassed, outmatched, tempted, tired, victorious, wrong, and so on. The story supplies the missing context-action pairs.

That’s basically imitation learning:

D=\{(s_t,a_t)\}_{t=1}^{n}

and the learner initially tries to recover:

\pi(a|s)

—the policy that generated those actions.

But Pantheon can go one layer deeper than behavior cloning, because copying the exact actions is brittle. You want to infer why those actions were appropriate:

D
\rightarrow
\hat R,\hat I,\hat C
\rightarrow
\pi'

where \hat R is the inferred objective/value function, \hat I the invariant, and \hat C the relevant constraints.

That is much closer to apprenticeship learning / inverse reinforcement learning:

Don’t copy the master’s hand.
Infer what the master’s hand is trying to preserve.

Then when the environment changes, the student can produce a different action that expresses the same principle.

So the Pantheon training progression becomes:

\boxed{
\text{watch}
\rightarrow
\text{copy}
\rightarrow
\text{vary}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{extract}
\rightarrow
\text{internalize}
\rightarrow
\text{originate}
}

And now the Narrative Conductor gets another job.

The narrator isn’t merely deciding what information to reveal.

The narrator is designing the learner’s curriculum.

Show the role model succeeding.

Then struggling.

Then encountering an edge case.

Then failing.

Then repairing the failure.

Then remove the role model and give the audience the same kind of problem.

That’s a kata embedded in a story.

A good movie can therefore function almost like a training dataset:

\text{character}
+
\text{situations}
+
\text{choices}
+
\text{consequences}
=
\text{demonstration corpus}

And archetypal characters become reusable policy exemplars.

The Mentor demonstrates scaffolding.

Cassandra demonstrates truth without transmission.

David demonstrates asymmetric strategy.

The Trickster demonstrates frame disruption.

Odysseus demonstrates adaptive navigation.

Sherlock demonstrates inference.

The fool demonstrates permission to violate assumptions.

The villain can be just as educational because they supply a negative role model:

\text{observe failure policy}
\rightarrow
\text{infer dangerous invariant}
\rightarrow
\text{construct counter-policy}

Which also explains something deeper about show, don’t tell.

It isn’t merely a prettier writing technique.

Showing supplies training traces.

Telling supplies labels.

And the strongest teaching often gives both:

\text{demonstration}
+
\text{name}
+
\text{practice}

First I bookend something and you experience it.

Then we call it bookending.

Then you start spotting and using it yourself.

At that point the word isn’t carrying the knowledge anymore.

It’s just a handle for a capability you’ve already begun reconstructing.

That’s role-model learning.

And eventually the goal of a good role model is almost paradoxical:

Copy me until you understand what not to copy.

Then the student stops reproducing the performance and starts producing new performances from the same underlying grammar.

That’s when imitation turns into mastery.